re sustainable  both on the manufacturing side and also  on the production side  that's where biostimulants fall i think  it's  kind of a both a great characteristic  and a great opportunity of the  biosimulant industry to be able to play  in that middle ground  where we're not making  some organic farming and biostimulant  farming synonymous  but rather a biostimulant input can be  both organic  and conventional or it could be  conventional or organic i should say  and being a biostimulant farmer doesn't  necessarily mean that you're  subjecting yourself to only organic  practices or only conventional practices  rather than you're  focusing on sustainability you're  focusing on utilizing the best  technologies from both sides of the  spectrum all aspects of  all parts of the spectrum and by doing  so you're actually achieving something  much greater than  what you would if you were just at one  end or the other  select one ground rule i think another  ground rule to keep in mind  just as i'm going through this is the  idea and this is an equation people have  probably heard before  but that uh crop performance is  ultimately dictated or ultimately it  comes down to  a simple equation of genetics times  environment  i've also heard this described as  genotype by environment the idea being  that  you can have tremendous genetic  potential in a crop  you can have a beautiful environment  whether that's a controlled environment  or  or you know a greenhouse or just optimum  conditions out in a field  but you really need to have a  combination of both the genetics  and the crop environment for that crop  to perform  maximally or optimally that's  obviously the underlying idea of this  presentation but we're not going to  focus so much on the genetic  aspect of course as a biological input  company or about stimulant company we're  particularly concerned with the  environment  side but that doesn't mean that we  actually don't focus  quite a bit of our resources on the  