g to an extent  that there are cultural movements that  are encouraging the the research and the  development and the production and the  use of biosignals  but it's also important to consider that  there's huge  you know global demands that consumers  aren't necessarily thinking of but that  us as academics researchers industry  folks  need to consider and need to start  preparing um  for the future more or less so  that being said biostimulants of today  are not what they were 20 years ago  so i'll say something like yield is easy  and i don't necessarily mean that in  doubling or tripling or quintupling a  yield is easy it's not  what i do mean by that is 20 years ago  when we had our first bacterial isolate  products and our first biostimulants  there were a lot of claims made like we  would increase yields you know 10 or 15  or 25 percent  and you could replicate that in the lab  but you could never replicate that  elsewhere  we've come a long way over the last 20  years and actually most biostimulant  products i'll take  microbial uh plant growth promoting  bacteria for example  their development their technology is so  far  superior nowadays to what it was 20  years ago that it's actually not  surprising at all  when a farmer who's not using a  biostimulant or a microbial inoculant  incorporates that product for the first  time for them to see a 5  10 15 increase in yield particularly if  it's into a system that's never  had any sort of input like that before  there's a replenishing aspect  you know especially out in the field  environment where adding even something  as simple as a growth hormone bacteria  can have these tremendous effects  also can have tremendous effects on  fertilizer reduction  so we've seen farmers reduce fertilizer  rates by 10  20 25 30 in some cases  and without any yield loss that's not  necessarily the direction that  biostimulant technology  is now focused on i think that's hugely  important but biomass yield in and of  itself  is